// Polkit rule letting the spoloh service account drive NetworkManager.
//
// Install to /etc/polkit-1/rules.d/50-spoloh-networkmanager.rules.
//
// Why this is needed: spoloh runs as User=spoloh (see spoloh.service), and
// NetworkManager's D-Bus API is polkit-gated. A system service has no active
// login session, so its requests fall to polkit's inactive/any defaults rather
// than the allow_active ones a desktop user gets. Without a rule, every nmcli
// call the daemon makes is denied, the access point never rises, and the board
// is exactly the unreachable device this whole feature exists to prevent.
//
// The three actions granted are the ones the daemon actually performs, and no
// others:
//
//   network-control        activating and deactivating a connection, which is
//                          `nmcli device wifi connect` and the hotspot going
//                          up and down
//   settings.modify.system creating the spoloh-hotspot connection profile and
//                          the profile a join writes; nmcli creates a system
//                          connection for both
//   wifi.scan              listing the networks in range for the setup page
//
// Deliberately not granted: enable-disable-network, enable-disable-wifi,
// sleep-wake, and anything touching the firewall or checkpoints. The daemon
// never asks for them, and a service that can announce air raid alerts should
// not also be able to switch the site's networking off.
//
// UNVERIFIED ON HARDWARE. This has not been run against a real board — no
// board was available while it was written, and nothing on a development
// machine can exercise polkit against NetworkManager. It is the standard form
// of this rule and the actions are read off the operations in
// internal/netmgr/nmcli.go, but treat it as a hypothesis until an install has
// confirmed it. If the daemon logs "could not read the network status"
// forever on a board that does have NetworkManager, this file is the first
// place to look: check `journalctl -u polkit` for a denial naming one of the
// actions above.
//
// polkit >= 0.106 reads this JavaScript format. Distributions still shipping
// polkit 0.105 (Debian bullseye and older) ignore rules.d entirely and need
// the equivalent as a .pkla file under
// /etc/polkit-1/localauthority/50-local.d/ instead. The installer is
// responsible for choosing; see Plan 3b.

polkit.addRule(function (action, subject) {
    if (subject.user !== "spoloh") {
        return polkit.Result.NOT_HANDLED;
    }
    if (action.id === "org.freedesktop.NetworkManager.network-control" ||
        action.id === "org.freedesktop.NetworkManager.settings.modify.system" ||
        action.id === "org.freedesktop.NetworkManager.wifi.scan") {
        return polkit.Result.YES;
    }
    return polkit.Result.NOT_HANDLED;
});
